{0 Frontend}

The Kairos frontend is split into two syntax levels.

{[
text file
  -> lexer / parser
  -> Kx_surface_syntax.source
  -> Kx_elaborate.elaborate_source
  -> Kx_ast.program
  -> Kairos_to_model
  -> Verification_model.node_model
]}

The verification pipeline consumes the elaborated AST, not the surface AST.
This keeps source conveniences out of the core engine.

{1 Surface Syntax}

[Kx_surface_syntax] is the AST produced directly by the parser.
It keeps all frontend conveniences explicit:

- enum declarations and finite domains;
- indexed variable families such as [signal[Route]];
- bounded [forall] / [exists] over finite domains;
- local predicates;
- named actions;
- topology blocks;
- history aliases;
- statement-level [for] loops;
- LTL helper forms such as [maintainedUntil] and [whileRouteLocked].

This AST is useful for diagnostics and frontend tests, but it is not the AST
used by the verification engine.

{1 Elaboration}

[Kx_elaborate] lowers surface syntax to [Kx_ast].
The pass expands or removes the syntactic extensions:

- indexed variable families become scalar variables with stable names;
- bounded quantifiers become finite conjunctions or disjunctions;
- local predicates are expanded at their call sites;
- named actions and [for] loops are inlined into ordinary statements;
- topology blocks generate ordinary LTL safety guarantees;
- [maintainedUntil(a,b,c)] becomes [G(a -> X(b W c))];
- [whileRouteLocked(r,p)] becomes [G(routeLocked(r) -> X(p W routeReleased(r)))].

Elaboration does not add a compatibility layer. Unknown predicates are rejected
instead of being preserved as implicit uninterpreted predicates.

{1 Core Frontend AST}

[Kx_ast] is the small AST accepted by the rest of the Kairos
implementation. It contains ordinary declarations, scalar variables, ordinary
statements, invariants and LTL contracts. It no longer contains surface
constructs such as [SSFor], [SSActionCall], [SCTopology], [SHForall] or
frontend predicate declarations.

Some constructs are real core-language features rather than pure syntax sugar:

- enum types and enum literals;
- pure first-order functions with contracts;
- ghost variables.

Pure functions are non-historical. Their contracts and bodies cannot mention
[pre], [preK] or local predicates. They are intended to translate directly to
Why3 functions.

In LTL and first-order formulas, a direct boolean call [p(x)] accepts identifier
arguments. For indexed references or general expressions, inject an executable
expression into the formula with braces, for example
[{ isIdleState(routeState[r]) } = true].

{1 HPred}

[HPred] is still present in the core logical expression type. It denotes an
explicit predicate application at the core level, and some lower layers still
know how to carry it.

The Kairos source frontend does not create [HPred] as a fallback for an
undeclared source predicate. A source predicate must be declared locally and is
expanded by [Kx_elaborate]; otherwise parsing/elaboration fails. This
prevents accidental proof obligations over names that the program never
defined.

{1 Debug Dumps}

The CLI exposes frontend boundary dumps:

- [--dump-surface=FILE] writes the parsed [Kx_surface_syntax.source];
- [--dump-elaborated=FILE] writes the elaborated source with [Kx_ast];
- [--dump-normalized-program=FILE] writes the normalized program after the
  later frontend/model pipeline.

Use these dumps to check whether a language extension is still surface syntax
or has reached the core engine.

{1 Main Modules}

- [Kx_parser] and [Kx_lexer]: source parser.
- [Kx_surface_syntax]: parser output AST.
- [Kx_elaborate]: surface-to-core elaboration.
- [Kx_ast]: elaborated frontend AST consumed by the pipeline.
- [Kx_parse_api]: public parsing API and JSON debug serialization.
- [Kairos_to_model]: lowering from frontend AST to the verification
  model.
